    <thead><tr><th>Mix-up</th><th>The fact</th><th>Converter</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Squaring a length factor</td><td>1 m = 3.28 ft, but 1 m² = 10.76 ft²</td><td><a href="../../calculators/area.html">Area</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fluid ounces vs. ounces</td><td>A fluid ounce is volume; an ounce is weight</td><td><a href="../../calculators/volume.html">Volume</a>, <a href="../../calculators/weight.html">weight</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>U.S. vs. UK gallons</td><td>The imperial gallon is about 20% larger</td><td><a href="../../calculators/volume.html">Volume</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Decimal feet</td><td>5.5 ft is 5 ft 6 in, not 5 ft 5 in</td><td><a href="../../calculators/length.html">Length</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Temperature differences</td><td>A 10°C rise is an 18°F rise, not 50°F</td><td><a href="../../calculators/temperature.html">Temperature</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Troy vs. everyday ounces</td><td>A troy ounce is about 9.7% heavier</td><td><a href="../../calculators/weight.html">Weight</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Keep the precision you started with</h2>
    <p>Converting doesn't make a measurement more precise. A room measured to the nearest inch is not accurate to a tenth of a millimeter after conversion; a recipe that calls for "about 1 cup" doesn't need 236.588 mL. Round the result to match the precision of the original measurement, and round up when you are buying material, so a conversion never leaves you short.</p>

    <h2>Weight vs. volume</h2>
    <p>Converting between weight and volume, such as cups of flour to grams or gallons of water to pounds, requires knowing the material's density, which these converters don't assume. Water is the convenient exception: a U.S. gallon of water weighs about 8.34 lb, and a liter of water weighs about 1 kilogram.</p>
  </div>
